\section{What is asked of the data}{
\code{x} is the false positive rate and \code{y} the true positive rate, both
fractions of one, in the order the curve is to be walked -- from (0, 0)
up, as \code{sklearn.metrics.roc_curve()} returns them, or from (1, 1) down, as
\code{pROC::coords()} does; the area is measured over the points sorted by \code{x}
either way. Several classifiers on one chart are several groups, split by
\code{colour}, \code{linetype} or \code{group} as a multi-series line is, and each is
announced by its group's name.
}

\section{Curves maidr reads without a declaration}{
Two idioms name their axes after the ROC's own vocabulary, and are read
as ROC curves as they stand: \code{\link[pROC:ggroc]{pROC::ggroc()}}, whose \code{geom_line()} maps
\code{specificity} (or \code{1-specificity} with \code{legacy.axes = TRUE}) against
\code{sensitivity}, and \code{ggplot2::autoplot()} of a \code{\link[yardstick:roc_curve]{yardstick::roc_curve()}},
whose \code{geom_path()} maps \code{1 - specificity} against \code{sensitivity}. Where
\code{x} is \code{specificity} itself -- pROC's default, drawn on a reversed axis --
the announced rate is \code{1 - specificity} and the axis is named so, because
the height above chance is measured against the false positive rate and a
rate read off a reversed axis would put every point on the wrong side of
the diagonal. Neither idiom carries thresholds or the area into the plot,
so the area is measured from the points and no threshold is announced;
\code{maidr_roc()} is how an author supplies both.
}

\section{Until the bundled maidr.js carries the trace}{
The \code{roc} trace shipped in maidr.js 4.9.0. While the copy this package
bundles is older (see \code{maidr:::MAIDR_VERSION}), a declared or detected
curve is read as a line, so that every chart keeps rendering; the
reading switches to \code{roc} with the next bundle update and no change to
the chart.
}

\section{What it costs not to declare}{
A \code{geom_line()} of rates under any other column names keeps the line
reading it has today, deliberately: every chart already written keeps
exactly the reading it has.
}

